      "completion_tokens": 1154,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion) via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant bonding is responsible for almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phases)\nWater is one of the few substances that exists naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Freezes at **0°C (32°F)** at standard pressure. Uniquely, ice is **less dense than liquid water** (it expands ~9% upon freezing), causing it to float. This insulates bodies of water, allowing life to survive underneath.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C. It has a high **surface tension**, high **specific heat capacity** (resists temperature change), and high **heat of vaporization** (requires lots of energy to boil).\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** at standard pressure. It is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Anomalous Properties (Why Water is \"Weird\")\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid due to hydrogen bonding:\n*   **Density Maximum at 4°C:** Liquid water is densest at 4°C, not at its freezing point. This causes lakes to freeze from the top down.\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Its polarity allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, acids, bases, gases), facilitating chemical reactions and nutrient transport.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** It can absorb massive amounts of heat with little temperature change, stabilizing Earth’s climate and organism body temperatures.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Allows capillary action (water moving up plant roots/stems) and supports small insects (water striders).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **Medium of Life:** All known biochemical reactions occur in aqueous solution.\n*   **Transport:** Blood (mostly water) transports nutrients, oxygen, and waste.\n*   **Thermoregulation:** Sweating/evaporative cooling prevents overheating.\n*   **Structural Support:** Turgor pressure in plant cells keeps them rigid.\n*   **Reactant:** Essential for **hydrolysis** (breaking down molecules) and **photosynthesis** (6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Distribution (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **~71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water.\n*   **97.5% is Saline** (oceans).\n*   **2.5% is Freshwater**, of which:\n    *   ~68.7% is locked in ice caps/glaciers.\n    *   ~30.1% is groundwater.\n    *   **< 1.2% is surface water** (lakes, rivers, swamps) — the primary source for human use.\n\n---\n\n### 6. The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle)\nWater is constantly moving through a closed system powered by the sun:\n1.  **Evaporation/Transpiration** (liquid → gas).\n2.  **Condensation** (gas → liquid clouds).\n3.  **Precipitation** (rain, snow).\n4.  **Collection/Runoff/Infiltration** (flowing back to oceans or aquifers).\n\n---\n\n### 7. Human Significance\n*   **Consumption:** Humans can survive only ~3 days without it.\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals are for irrigation.\n*   **Industry:** Cooling, processing, cleaning, hydroelectric power.\n*   **Sanitation:** Critical for hygiene and disease prevention.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite abundance, *accessible* freshwater is a finite, unevenly distributed resource under increasing stress from pollution and climate change.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nWater is **H₂O**—a small, polar molecule whose hydrogen bonding creates macroscopic properties (floating ice, high heat capacity, solvent power) that make it the **solvent, transport medium, thermal buffer, and structural foundation for all life on Earth.**"
